# binutils -- generated by tools/gen-recipes.sh, safe to edit.
# Source pins come from ../versions.env so a bump is one file.

SRC_URL=$BINUTILS_URL
SRC_SHA256=$BINUTILS_SHA256
SRC_DIR=$BINUTILS_SRCDIR

CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-nls --enable-gold --enable-ld=default --enable-plugins --enable-64-bit-bfd --enable-new-dtags --enable-default-hash-style=gnu --with-system-zlib --without-zstd --disable-werror --enable-gprofng=no --enable-deterministic-archives"

# --enable-deterministic-archives makes ar zero the timestamp, uid, gid and mode
# in every member header by default.
#
# Without it, ar stamps the build time into each member, so two builds of the
# same source produce different .a files -- identical member lists, identical
# order, identical sizes, different bytes. That was the whole of glibc's
# non-determinism: 7 static archives out of 1463 files, with every shared
# library and binary already identical.
#
# It applies to every package that builds a static archive, not just glibc, and
# takes effect once binutils has been rebuilt with it and lands in the builder
# image.

# --without-zstd IS LOAD-BEARING AND LOOKS DECORATIVE, which is why it is
# written down here.
#
# binutils 2.45's --with-zstd DEFAULTS TO AUTO: configure probes for libzstd and
# links it if it finds one. On a tree with no zstd package that probe fails,
# nothing links, and the flag appears to do nothing at all. The moment zstd
# exists in the build image the probe succeeds and `as` acquires a runtime
# dependency ON A LIBRARY NO RECIPE EVER DECLARED.
#
# That is not hypothetical, and the timings say something stronger than an
# ordering mistake. binutils and zstd BUILT AT THE SAME LEVEL, overlapping --
# binutils 18:02:03-18:07:55, zstd 18:07:18-18:10:23 -- so NO BUILD ORDER COULD
# EVER HAVE SEPARATED THEM. The edge is invisible to dep-levels.sh BY
# CONSTRUCTION, because it is created by a probe at configure time rather than
# by anything a recipe declares. Measured on the artefact that climb produced:
# `as` had 1 reference to libzstd.so, against 1 for libz and 3 for libc as
# controls. The published binutils has none, in either /usr/bin/as or the
# target-triple copy.
#
# DECLARING zstd INSTEAD WOULD BE WORSE: binutils would then depend on zstd, and
# zstd needs binutils to build -- a permanent cycle that dep-levels.sh would
# refuse to produce a graph for. Refusing the capability at the source is what
# breaks it, and compressed debug sections in zstd format are not something
# anything here needs. --with-system-zlib on the same line is the deliberate
# opposite: zlib IS declared, so its probe is allowed to succeed.
